 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of voxels intersected by the ray. In the worst case (ray passing through the entire grid), this is proportional to the grid dimension in the ray's direction.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(W \times H \times D)$ for the voxel grid storage.
-*   **Performance:** Fast for dense, uniform volumes. Performance degrades significantly in sparse scenes compared to hierarchical methods like [Sparse Voxel Traversal](1525-sparse-voxel-traversal.md) because it cannot skip empty space [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Simple implementation, no build time for acceleration structures, constant-time access to any voxel, suitable for dynamic scenes where the grid changes frequently [S2, S6, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** High memory usage for large sparse volumes, inability to skip empty space, precision issues near voxel boundaries if not handled carefully (e.g., epsilon offsets) [S1, S2, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** Dense voxel data, real-time applications with limited preprocessing time, or when the scene is fully occupied [S2, unofficial].
-*   **When not to use:** Sparse scenes, large worlds with significant empty space, or when memory bandwidth is a bottleneck [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of voxels intersected by the ray. In the worst case (ray passing through the entire grid), $N$ is proportional to the grid dimension.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(V)$ where $V$ is the total number of voxels in the grid. The storage is typically a single 1D array or 3D texture [S1, S9, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** Performance is highly dependent on grid density and ray direction. Axis-aligned rays are fastest as they traverse fewer voxels per unit distance. Diagonal rays traverse more voxels. The method is very fast on GPUs due to coalesced memory access patterns when traversing linear buffers or 3D textures [S9, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Simple implementation, no complex data structure traversal overhead (like BVH/Octree node pointers), efficient for dense uniform grids, easy to parallelize on GPU [S1, S2, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Inefficient for sparse scenes (many empty voxels traversed), fixed resolution (aliasing artifacts at high zoom), memory usage scales with the cube of the grid dimension [S1, S2, unofficial].
+*   **When to use:** Dense voxel volumes, uniform grids, real-time rendering where simplicity and parallelism are key [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When not to use:** Highly sparse scenes (use Sparse Voxel Traversal or Octree), scenes with complex continuous geometry (use Ray-Primitive intersection) [S2, S3, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The method is based on the Digital Differential Analyzer (DDA) algorithm for ray traversal, originally described by Amanatides and Woo in 1987 [S3, unofficial]. It is widely used in voxel-based games (e.g., *Teardown*, *Minecraft* rendering optimizations) and real-time ray tracing demos [S1, unofficial]. The distinction between Voxel-Volume-Raycasting and general Volume-Raycasting is that voxels are defined as integer-coordinate functions $f: \mathbb{N}^3 \to U$, whereas general volume raycasting may use floating-point sampling and interpolation [S3, unofficial].
+The method is based on the Digital Differential Analyzer (DDA) algorithm for ray tracing, originally described by Amanatides and Woo in 1987 [S3, unofficial]. It is a fundamental technique in voxel-based rendering, often used in games and real-time applications for collision detection and volume visualization [S1, S5, unofficial]. The approach distinguishes itself from general volume raycasting by treating voxels as discrete integer-valued entities rather than continuous functions, requiring explicit floor operations for coordinate mapping [S3, unofficial].
 
